if we can talk to people as  humans first  then we can start to change the stigma  and help people find the resources when  they're ready and  help them to maintain their recovery so  one quick example that i hear often and  is kind of the most common is changing  from addict or addiction and dirty to a  person with a substance use disorder or  a person who tests positive for  substances  if we're talking about testing and those  types of things so again if you notice  this person first language you put the  person first so you say a person with  substance use disorder a person with  opioid use disorder  former addict getting clean right those  are another example of some stigmatizing  phrases that people  often use and instead you would say a  person  in recovery or someone who is testing  negative for substances  clean sober right a lot of times we have  these negative association words that  come with it implying that someone with  opioid use disorder or substance use  disorder  is dirty uh or  immoral or something like that and we  can really shift our language to saying  substance free  treatment is the goal  it doesn't sound stigmatizing  at the onset right but honestly we can  shift our language to say treatment is  one path to recovery not every person  wants to or can engage in treatment so  it is one path and recovery does not  look the same for all people it is  there's not one size fits all for a  recovery path so that's something that's  really important to remember as well  and opioid replacement or opioid  management we don't like to use those  words we would focus more on medication  assisted treatment or medication for  opioid use disorder m-o-u-d or m-a-t  are some common phrases that are  non-stigmatizing  and lastly one that is used often is  relapse and that can be really  stigmatizing to people who are in  recovery so what we would say instead  and kind of framing this to people with  say recurrence or return return to  substance use so replacing some of those  common phrases that we've heard over and  over again to phrases that are more  person-first and compassionate and  empathetic to encourage recovery and  reduce stigma and it's okay if we've  made these mistakes in the past our goal  is to recognize and be mindful and shift  them in the future and start to show  people that we can change this language  it's definitely possible  the next piece that i wanted to talk  about specifically is evidence-based  treatment so  treatment does work it's effective it's  evidence-based and recovery is possible  treatment works best when you have a  whole person approach that includes the  biopsychosocial model so that includes  things like biological backgrounds  injuries genetics those type of things  psychological history so  any um  triggers coping skills where people are  at right now past traumas and then  social uh settings and social things  like family life and communities that  they're in peers and these all have  interactions that are very complex  there's not one single  thing or aspect of a person that causes  a substance use disorder it's not a  simple issue there's all these things  that are interactive so when we're  talking about treatment and recovery we  want to make sure that we're talking  about a whole person because that is  what's most effective and some specific  examples of some evidence-based  treatment models include cognitive  behavioral therapy that focuses on  thoughts behaviors and feelings and  increasing coping skills and replacing  some of those negative thoughts or  things that are no longer serving the  person with more adaptive skills  motivational interviewing which includes  non-judgmental interviews and guiding  the person helping them to understand  their own goals  and motivations  mindfulness-based stress reduction or  mbsr which includes mindfulness and  awareness and helping the person to  identify triggers and engaging in  healthy coping skills in those moments  and lastly just on our quick list of  examples here this is not an exhaustive  list is acceptance and commitment  therapy or act  and that is really values based and  focuses on a lot of mindfulness and  being present in the moment and using  skills and replacing some of those  skills that are healthy with things that  you can use in the moment and not using  the unhealthy skills or things that you  have used in the past for coping such as  substances  again briefly talking about moud  medications for opioid use disorder when  you combine these with behavioral  counseling and some of those  evidence-based treatments that i  mentioned for a whole patient approach  it reduces opioid overdose deaths  substantially it's evidence-based and  it's very important for people to get  into those treatments if it's something  that they would like to do so stigma  reduction is one way that we can  encourage recovery and effective  evidence-based treatment and talking  about medication for opioid use disorder  is another way that we can help people  to understand these evidence-based  treatments for individuals with opioid  use disorder as well  and talking about them in ways that we  can really increase their acceptance and  um use because they are one of our harm  reduction tools and evidence-based ways  for treating opioid use disorder  including things like methadone  buprenorphine or suboxone and naltrexone  and so i'm just going to continue on and  talking about oh and also vivitrol um  with that so  talking about some key takeaways here  after we've reviewed the opioid crisis  stigma and how to reduce it and then  some evidence-based treatments for  opioid use disorder i think it really  shows that we are all in this together  it is very common for people to start  taking opioid medications and  i know several people who have had  babies and been prescribed opioid  medications after their c-section and  they continue to take those medications  even though they may or may not need  them but their doctor prescribes them so  they're going to take them because they  were under the impression that they just  keep taking them and that's something  that they're supposed to do until  they're gone and after about five to  seven days people can become dependent  on their opioid medications and so we're  really  all in this together from moms doctors  right everybody can play a role in  preventing opioid use disorder from  yourself from others medical providers  each of us has a role to play  in reducing opioid use disorder and  stigma and i want you to think just one  thing that you can do and i have a few  examples of some things that you can do  today after this presentation some  action items for you and thinking about  that mom or right that person who you  know that loved one that child who broke  their arm and is exposed to opioids what  are some things that we can do we can  really focus on prevention so if you  don't need an opioid medication don't  take one talk to your doctor and if you  are a medical provider talk to your  patients about their past risk factors  are they somebody with a substance use  disorder maybe an opioid medication is  not right for them where some other type  of medication would be right or doing  some type of alternative  pain management therapy so things like  cognitive behavioral therapy are really  effective for managing pain or  mindfulness based  intervention so not taking opioids is  one  one factor in reducing that risk so is  medication disposal and i mentioned at  the beginning that 50 of people who  misuse opioid medication get them from a  loved one so getting those medications  out of your house and into the correct  drop boxes is really important we can  also talk about harm reduction as  something that you can discuss and do  today right so reducing those  undesirable consequences to make a path  for when people are ready for recovery  so things like naloxone where if  somebody has an overdose naloxone can  reverse the effects of that overdose  temporarily so something that's really  important is to  provide and to have on hand naloxone or  narcan for people who do get opioid  prescriptions or who are using illicit  opiates  like heroin or fentanyl because when  people survive they do go into recovery  and you can't recover if you overdose  and you are not revived so that's  something that's really important that i  want to hit home is that harm reduction  is another evidence-based way to help  support people into recovery  and then treatment and recovery like i  mentioned there's no one-stop shop it  takes a lot of time something you can do  today is reaching out to a loved one or  somebody that you care about saying hey  how's it going checking in offering to  take them to a appointment or give  giving them resources and i have a list  of national resources coming up towards  the end here that can help get you  started in finding resources to provide  to a loved one and offering them that  support and help when they're ready on  their path to recovery and finally  something that seems simple but makes a  really big difference is sharing  teaching others about what you've  learned today about stigma reduction and  evidence-based treatment  one example of that is the stories of  utah's opioid crisis we have some  wonderful  qualitative interviews that are all  housed online that you're welcome to  search and listen to that talk a lot  about recovery and hope and how people  found that pathway specifically in utah  so we'd love to have people look at that  and other national opioid use disorder  resources these are some common lists of  places that i like to send uh people to  to just get an idea of okay i have a  loved one who needs support where can i  send them the american psychological  association or apa has a lot of great  substance use related resources so if  you click or search that link  that would be a wonderful place to start  the cdc also has a lot 